# Metadata and serialized assets

- NEVER hand-write or hand-edit `.meta`, `.unity`, `.prefab`, or `.asset` files. GUIDs and fileIDs
  are Unity's to mint, and copying a `.meta` from another asset duplicates its GUID and silently
  breaks every reference to it.
- Never generate a GUID yourself. If a sidecar is missing or malformed, delete only that sidecar,
  let Unity regenerate it, then confirm the new `guid:` is 32 hex characters and unique.
- Importing needs no CLI. Files placed under `Assets/` are imported by the running Editor on focus,
  and it writes the `.meta` itself. Prefer this whenever an import is all you need.

# Resolving Unity CLI problems

Work down this list. Never skip ahead to editing files by hand, and never treat a blocked CLI as
permission to author metadata yourself — that holds when the CLI is missing entirely, not just when
it is failing. No step below is a prerequisite for importing: files placed under `Assets/` are
imported by the open Editor with no CLI involved at all, so reach for that before you consider the
task blocked.

1. Confirm the binary exists before reading anything into a failure:
   `which unity && unity --version`. A `command not found` is not proof it is absent. The Unity Hub
   installs the CLI automatically, so it is usually present but not yet on `PATH` — a fresh shell
   resolves that, most often on Windows — and a sandbox can hide it exactly as in step 4. Once it
   responds, `unity doctor` checks `PATH` presence and whether a second `unity` binary shadows the
   real one.
2. If it is genuinely absent, ask the user to install it rather than installing it yourself. The
   documented install pipes a script from Unity's CDN into a shell and writes a binary onto their
   machine outside this project, so that is their call, not yours — unlike the project-scoped
   package in step 6. The commands are in the `/unity-cli` skill. A new shell is needed afterwards,
   so you will likely not be able to use it until the next session.
3. Run `unity pipeline list`. It is the one command that works without the
   `com.unity.pipeline` package, and it reports each instance's `isRunning`, `hasPipelinePackage`,
   `pipelineServer.isReachable`, and `safeMode`.
4. Rule out sandbox artifacts before believing a failure. `The required instruction sets are not
   supported by the current CPU.` and `unity status` reporting no instances are both commonly
   produced by a restrictive agent sandbox hiding a healthy Editor. Re-run the same command with
   elevated permissions before concluding anything is unavailable.
5. Safe Mode means C# compile errors, and `unity pipeline list` reports it. Fixing the source is the
   fix, not a workaround; the Editor must be restarted afterwards.
6. A missing `com.unity.pipeline` package blocks `unity status`, `unity command`, and `unity list`.
   Installing it is Unity's documented setup step, not a workaround: run `unity auth login`, then
   `unity pipeline install`, wait for the Editor to recompile, then confirm with
   `unity pipeline list`. Take the default version rather than pinning one, and pass
   `--project-path` when several projects are open, because non-interactive runs refuse to guess.
   The package resolves from the Unity registry into `Packages/manifest.json`, which is tracked —
   say that you changed it, and keep it out of unrelated commits.
7. Batch mode refuses a project that is already open (`already open in a running Editor (PID ...)`).
   Ask the user to close the Editor instead of working around it, and never run `-executeMethod`
   against the project they have open.
8. If none of that unblocks you, stop and say plainly what you tried and what you need — install the
   CLI, close the Editor, sign in, or enable Unity MCP. Do not improvise a substitute path.

- Refer to the `/unity-cli` skill for more information regarding Unity CLI, including how to detect
  and install it. Installation is documented at
  [Use the Unity CLI](https://docs.unity.com/en-us/unity-cli/use-unity-cli), and the Pipeline
  package at
  [Unity Pipeline package](https://docs.unity.com/en-us/unity-production-pipeline/local-tools-cli/unity-pipeline-package).

# Assets

- For prototyping assets, use the `/asset-prototype` skill — it sources from the team-owned
  `music-cell-shared-assets` repository. Do not download third-party asset packs.
- For sprites, either width and height must be multiple of 4, or pack them in SpriteAtlas, or else no compression is applied.
- Provide visual checks for users to confirm if the assets look what user is expecting for.
- For fixed Unity UI, author containers, dimensions, alignment, and visual styling in prefabs and use serialized prefab references for repeated elements; do not derive sizing from runtime RectTransforms or force layout rebuilds unless requirements explicitly call for dynamic layout.
